$DIR/main.p:9:35: v was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by value) at line 11, but the deferred function (line 10) uses it when the function returns
    L is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. Passing an owner by value moves it instead of copying it, so
    that what it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 11, v holds nothing. The
    function fs[0], which holds a function literal that uses v, is deferred at
    line 10 and runs when the function returns, after line 11, so it would use
    what v no longer holds.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Give v a new value after line 11, so that the deferred call finds one:
        v = L{}
    Or do the work of the deferred call at the end of the function, before line
    11, instead of deferring it
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)
